   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-12-03T13:40:13Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>What if I'm programming on DOS, and I have a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Far_pointer\" rel=\"noreferrer\">far pointer</a> (one composed of a segment and an offset), and it's pointing to the last address in the segment, and I add one to it, and the pointer <a href=\"http://www.os2museum.com/wp/who-needs-the-address-wraparound-anyway/\" rel=\"noreferrer\">wraps around</a>?  It looks like when you're comparing them, you normalize the pointers, so the second pointer <code>p+1</code> would be less than <code>p</code>.</p>\n\n<p>This is a stab in the dark though, I don't have a DOS C compiler handy to test on.</p>\n"
  },
