   "score": 9,
   "created_utc": "2014-12-03T14:30:12Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "@barakmanos The question is quite clear, and it is about what the language guarantees you. The language has clear definitions as for what + and &lt; mean for a pointer variable, it&#39;s quite irrelevant what akarapatis definition of &lt; for a pointer is, or whether you consider pointers as integers."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 43018061,
   "parent_id": 27273207,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 15416,
   "author_display_name": "MSalters",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/27272992/#comment43018061_27273207",
   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2014-12-03T14:38:00Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Not entirely. Depending on the memory model, you can have objects which exceed segmentation boundaries. In the <code>huge</code> memory model, incrementing a pointer to the last address in a segment is valid because arrays there can cross segment boundaries. That means the compiler must check for overflow and adjust the segment part accordingly. With unsegmented pointers, you can&#39;t wrap around across <code>nullptr</code>."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 27274515,
   "parent_id": 27272992,
   "author_user_id": 841108,
   "author_display_name": "Basile Starynkevitch",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/27274515",
   "score": 10,
   "created_utc": "2014-12-03T14:44:05Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2014-12-03T16:43:20Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-12-03T16:43:20Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>It could happen with an invalid pointer.</p>\n\n<p>But if the pointer points to a valid memory location, on many operating systems (e.g. Linux), it <em>practically never happens</em> (at least if the <code>sizeof(*p)</code> is not too big), because <em>in practice</em> the first and last pages of the address space are never mapped (but you could force a mapping with <code>mmap</code> &amp; <code>MAP_FIXED</code>).</p>\n\n<p>For freestanding implementations (i.e. <em>inside</em> a kernel, or on some microcontroller), things are different, and implementation specific (perhaps might be <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undefined_behavior\" rel=\"noreferrer\">undefined behavior</a>, or <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unspecified_behavior\" rel=\"noreferrer\">unspecified behavior</a>).</p>\n"
  },
  {
