  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 27275978,
   "parent_id": 27272992,
   "author_user_id": 3255455,
   "author_display_name": "gnasher729",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/27275978",
   "score": 10,
   "created_utc": "2014-12-03T15:50:54Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2014-12-03T15:50:54Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Very simple: It cannot happen if there is no undefined behaviour involved. It can happen very easily in the presence of undefined behaviour. For details, read a copy of the C Standard or C++ Standard. </p>\n\n<p>As a result, a conforming compiler is allowed to not evaluate the &lt; operator at all and use 1 or true as the result instead. The same is true for arithmetic with signed integers (but not for unsigned integers, where it is possible for entirely legal code to have x > x+1).</p>\n\n<p>Your example code isn't even C or C++, so you seem to have used the compiler in a mode where it isn't a standard conforming C or C++ compiler. </p>\n"
  },
